# Example Azure Kubernetes Service load balancer configuration

This example shows how to configure an example LoadBalancer service into Azure AKS.

## Prerequisites

* You must have an existing AKS cluster.
* You must have a public IP address in your subscription.
* You must have an existing load balancer resource in your subscription.

**Note:** If you don't already have an existing AKS cluster, you can create one by following the instructions in [Creating an AKS cluster](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/aks/tutorial-kubernetes-deploy-application).

You'll also need to go through the process of creating a public IP address and load balancer resource first. For more information about creating resources using Azure CLI commands, see [Using Azure CLI commands for managing Azure resources](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/cli/azure/manage-azure-resources?view=azure-cli-latest#getting-started-with-resource-groups-and-subscriptions).

## Configure an AKS load balancer service

Follow the steps below to configure the AKS load balancer service:

### Create a new AKS cluster

If you don't already have an existing AKS cluster, follow these steps to create one:
```bash
az group create --location westus --name myResourceGroup

az aks create \
    --resource-group=myResourceGroup \
    --name=myAKSCluster \
    --node-count=1 \
    --generate-ssh-keys \
    --kubernetes-version=1.29.2 \
    --enable-addons monitoring \
    --network-plugin azure
```
Once the command completes successfully, output will look similar to the following:
```
{
  "aadProfile": null,
  "addonProfiles": {
    "monitoring": {
      "config": {
        "clusterMonitoring": true,
        "kubeDashboard": true
      },
      "disabled": false,
      "enabled": true
    },
    "security": {
      "config": null,
      "disabled": false,
      "enabled": true
    }
  },
  "agentPoolProfiles": [
    {
      "count": 1,
      "maxPods": 110,
      "mode": "System",
      "osDiskSizeGB": 30,
      "osType": "Linux",
      "provisioningState": "Succeeded",
      "restartAction": "TerminationGracePeriod",
      "vmSize": "Standard_DS2_v2"
    }
  ],
  "availabilityProfile": "Manual",
  "disableRbac": null,
  "dnsPrefix": "myakscluster",
  "fqUse CTRL+C to exit
```

The default `kubectl` context will now switch to the load-balancer service you've configured.

